# 50. AUTHORING RULES

When creating Seminar III instructional content:

1. Explain concepts literally before introducing memory aids.
2. Avoid figures of speech when they could make the concept less clear.
3. Define unfamiliar vocabulary.
4. Use examples that make sense independently.
5. Use practical examples without assuming prior life experience.
6. Model the reasoning process, not only the final calculation.
7. Include an "If You Forget" strategy when appropriate.
8. Include retrieval from previous learning.
9. Separate skill learning from ACT strategy.
10. Move from supported practice toward independent application.
11. Ask students to check whether answers make sense.
12. Require evidence for reading and data conclusions.
13. Connect academic skills to practical applications.
14. Do not require postsecondary exploration every week.
15. Use life-skills instruction as an additional opportunity to apply academic content.
16. Use "Learner" (capital L), not "student," in curriculum copy shown directly to Learners: instructional pages, printable practice/check sheets, and interactive (H5P) content. Matches `../../02-authoring-system/content-voice-and-tone.md`'s FoxCS-wide rule, adopted for Seminar III 2026-08-29 (previously used "student" throughout; converted). Internal/design documents -- this file, `01_SEMINAR_III_COURSE_PLAN`, the `unit-NN-plan` source docs -- and teacher-only material (`teacher-materials/` decks and answer keys) can still say "student," same carve-out the voice doc gives grading rubrics and teacher-only keys generally.

